export type Essay = { id: string; type: string; question: string; answer: string; notes: string[] };
export const TASK2_ESSAYS: Essay[] = [
  { id: "community-service", type: "Opinion (agree or disagree)",
    question: "Some people believe that unpaid community service should be a compulsory part of high school programmes. To what extent do you agree or disagree?",
    notes: ["Clear position in the introduction and conclusion", "One paragraph for benefits, one that concedes and answers the opposing view", "Specific examples: care homes, coaching, 40 hours over two years"],
    answer: "Many schools now encourage students to volunteer, and some educators argue that community service should be made compulsory. I largely agree with this view, as long as the scheme is flexible and well organised.\n\nThe main argument for compulsory service is that it develops qualities that classroom lessons rarely teach. When teenagers help in a care home, coach younger children or clean up a local park, they learn responsibility, teamwork and empathy for people whose lives differ from their own. These experiences can also help them choose a career; a student who volunteers at a hospital, for example, may discover a genuine interest in nursing. Moreover, if service is optional, it tends to attract only students who are already confident and well connected, so making it compulsory gives every young person the same opportunity.\n\nAdmittedly, critics point out that forced volunteering is a contradiction and that students already face heavy academic pressure. A teenager preparing for final examinations may resent losing several hours a week, and an unwilling volunteer is unlikely to help anyone. However, these problems can be reduced through careful design. Schools could require a modest number of hours, perhaps 40 over two years, and let students choose projects that match their interests and timetables.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that community service should be a required part of secondary education, because its benefits for both students and society outweigh the inconvenience. Provided that schools keep the commitment reasonable and offer real choice, compulsory service can become something students value rather than resent." },
  { id: "crime", type: "Discuss both views and give your opinion",
    question: "Some people think that the best way to reduce crime is to give longer prison sentences. Others, however, believe there are better alternative ways of reducing crime. Discuss both views and give your opinion.",
    notes: ["Both views get a full paragraph", "The writer's opinion is stated in the introduction and repeated in the conclusion", "Cause and effect linking: furthermore, which makes … more likely"],
    answer: "Crime is a concern in every society, and there is disagreement about how best to tackle it. While some argue that longer prison sentences are the most effective deterrent, I believe that prevention and rehabilitation offer better long-term results.\n\nSupporters of longer sentences make two main points. First, the threat of a long period in prison may discourage people from committing crimes in the first place. Second, while dangerous offenders are in prison, they cannot harm the public, so communities are protected, at least temporarily. For serious violent crimes in particular, many people also feel that long sentences deliver justice to victims.\n\nOn the other hand, there is considerable evidence that harsher punishment alone does not reduce crime. Many offenders act on impulse or under the influence of drugs and do not weigh up the possible sentence. Furthermore, prisons can become 'schools of crime', where young offenders learn from more experienced criminals, and released prisoners often struggle to find work, which makes reoffending more likely. Alternatives such as education programmes, drug treatment and community sentences address the causes of criminal behaviour, and investment in youth centres and job training in deprived areas can stop young people from turning to crime at all.\n\nIn conclusion, although long prison terms are appropriate for the most dangerous criminals, they are an expensive and often ineffective way to reduce crime overall. In my view, governments should focus their resources on prevention and rehabilitation, which tackle the root causes of offending." },
  { id: "online-shopping", type: "Advantages outweigh disadvantages?",
    question: "Nowadays, many people choose to shop online rather than in stores. Do the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?",
    notes: ["Answers the question asked (outweigh?) in the introduction", "A third body paragraph explains why the drawbacks can be managed", "Conclusion weighs both sides and repeats the answer"],
    answer: "Online shopping has grown rapidly over the past decade, and for many people it has replaced visits to physical stores. Although this trend has some drawbacks, I believe its advantages are greater.\n\nThe most obvious benefit of online shopping is convenience. Customers can compare prices from dozens of sellers in minutes and order products at any time of day without travelling. This is particularly valuable for elderly or disabled people and for those living in rural areas with few shops. In addition, competition between online sellers tends to lower prices, and customer reviews help buyers avoid poor-quality products.\n\nHowever, the shift online has clear disadvantages. Traditional shops are closing, which reduces employment and makes town centres less lively. Shoppers also cannot touch or try products before buying, so many items are returned, which wastes packaging and fuel. There are also risks of fraud and data theft when people pay online.\n\nNevertheless, most of these problems can be managed. Many retailers now combine online sales with physical showrooms, allowing customers to see products before ordering, and secure payment systems have made fraud less common. Town centres can also adapt by offering experiences, such as cafés, entertainment and services, that cannot be delivered by post.\n\nIn conclusion, while online shopping has damaged some local businesses and brought new risks, its convenience, lower prices and accessibility make it a positive development overall, especially as businesses and towns adapt to it." },
  { id: "traffic", type: "Causes and solutions",
    question: "In many cities, traffic congestion is becoming a serious problem. What are the causes of this problem, and what measures could be taken to solve it?",
    notes: ["One paragraph of causes, one of solutions, matched to each other", "Sequencing: firstly, secondly, finally", "Each solution is explained, not just listed"],
    answer: "Traffic congestion is now a daily reality in many cities, wasting time and polluting the air. This essay will examine the main causes of this problem and suggest some possible solutions.\n\nThe primary cause of congestion is the growing number of private cars. As incomes rise, more families can afford a car, and many people prefer the comfort and flexibility of driving to using public transport. A second cause is poor urban planning. In many cities, homes are built far from workplaces, schools and shops, so residents must make long journeys every day. Where public transport is slow, crowded or unreliable, people have little choice but to drive.\n\nSeveral measures could reduce the problem. Firstly, governments should invest in fast, affordable public transport, such as metro systems and dedicated bus lanes, so that it becomes a realistic alternative to the car. Secondly, charging drivers to enter city centres at peak times, as some major cities already do, can discourage unnecessary journeys and raise money for public transport. Finally, planners could create mixed-use neighbourhoods where people live close to their jobs and services, and employers could offer flexible hours and remote work to spread journeys more evenly through the day.\n\nIn conclusion, congestion is mainly caused by rising car ownership and poorly planned cities. A combination of better public transport, road pricing and smarter planning offers the best chance of making city travel faster and cleaner." },
  { id: "working-abroad", type: "Two-part question",
    question: "Many young people today are leaving their home countries to work abroad. Why is this happening? Is it a positive or negative development?",
    notes: ["Both questions are answered, each in its own paragraph(s)", "Names the opposing effect (brain drain) before answering it", "Topic vocabulary: remittances, skilled professionals, international contacts"],
    answer: "An increasing number of young people are moving abroad for work. This essay will explain the reasons for this trend and argue that, on balance, it is a positive development.\n\nThere are several reasons why young people choose to work overseas. The most significant is economic: salaries in wealthier countries are often several times higher than at home, and some sectors, such as technology and healthcare, actively recruit foreign workers. In addition, many young graduates cannot find jobs that match their qualifications in their own country. Finally, cheaper flights and the internet have made moving abroad less daunting, as people can stay in touch with their families easily.\n\nThis trend has some negative effects, particularly for the home country. When doctors, engineers and other skilled professionals leave, their countries lose the benefit of the education they provided, a problem often called 'brain drain'. Families may also suffer from long separations.\n\nHowever, I believe the advantages are greater. Workers abroad often send part of their earnings home, and these payments support families and boost local economies. Moreover, many migrants eventually return with new skills, international contacts and savings to start businesses. Even those who remain abroad can strengthen trade and cultural links between countries.\n\nIn conclusion, young people move abroad mainly for better pay and career opportunities. Although this can cause a loss of talent in the short term, the financial support and skills that migrants bring back make it a largely positive trend." },
  { id: "living-alone", type: "Positive or negative development",
    question: "In some countries, more and more people are choosing to live alone. Is this a positive or negative development?",
    notes: ["Acknowledges the benefits before arguing the main view", "Three developed disadvantages with firstly, secondly, finally", "A recommendation in the conclusion"],
    answer: "In many countries, the number of one-person households has risen sharply in recent decades. While living alone brings certain benefits, I believe that, overall, it is a negative development for society.\n\nThere are understandable reasons why people choose to live alone, and some advantages to it. Higher incomes allow young professionals to afford their own flats, and many value the independence and privacy this brings. Living alone can encourage self-reliance, as people must manage their own finances and household tasks. For some, it also provides a calm environment in which to focus on their careers or hobbies.\n\nHowever, the disadvantages are more serious. Firstly, living alone often leads to loneliness, which research has linked to depression and poor physical health, especially among older people. Secondly, one-person households use resources less efficiently: each needs its own kitchen, heating and appliances, so more homes must be built and more energy is consumed per person. This increases demand for housing in cities that are already short of space and pushes up prices for everyone. Finally, when people live alone, there may be no one to help them in an emergency, such as a fall or sudden illness.\n\nIn conclusion, although living alone offers independence and freedom, it can harm people's wellbeing and place extra pressure on housing and the environment. Communities and governments should therefore encourage shared and intergenerational living, while providing support for those who live on their own." },
];

export type Letter = { id: string; tone: "Formal" | "Semi-formal" | "Informal"; prompt: string; bullets: string[]; answer: string };
export const GT_LETTERS: Letter[] = [
  { id: "complaint", tone: "Formal", prompt: "You recently bought an electrical appliance from a shop, but it does not work properly. Write a letter to the shop manager.",
    bullets: ["describe what you bought", "explain the problem", "say what you would like the manager to do"],
    answer: "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to complain about a coffee machine that I bought from your store on the High Street on 3 March. The model is the Barista Pro 200, and I paid £149 for it.\n\nUnfortunately, the machine has not worked properly since the first week. Although it heats water, it leaks heavily from the base every time I use it, leaving a large pool of water on the kitchen counter. I have carefully followed the instructions in the manual and cleaned the machine as recommended, but the problem has continued. I am also concerned that the leak could damage the electrical parts and make the machine unsafe.\n\nAs the machine is still under its one-year guarantee, I would be grateful if you could either replace it with a new one or give me a full refund. I have kept the receipt and the original box, and I can bring the machine to the store at your convenience.\n\nI look forward to hearing from you.\n\nYours faithfully,\nDaniel Clarke" },
  { id: "landlord", tone: "Semi-formal", prompt: "There is a problem with the heating in the flat you rent. Write a letter to your landlord.",
    bullets: ["describe the problem", "explain how it is affecting you", "say what you want the landlord to do"],
    answer: "Dear Mr Patel,\n\nI am writing about a problem with the heating in my flat at 14 Elm Road, which I have been renting from you since last September.\n\nFor the past ten days, the radiators in the bedroom and living room have stayed cold, even when the boiler is switched on. The hot water still works, so I think the fault may be with the heating pump or the thermostat, but I have not tried to repair anything myself.\n\nAs the temperature has dropped below freezing at night, the flat has become extremely cold. I have had to buy an electric heater, which is expensive to run, and I have found it difficult to work from home in the evenings. I am also worried that damp may develop on the walls.\n\nI would be grateful if you could arrange for a qualified engineer to inspect and repair the heating as soon as possible. I am at home on most weekday mornings, so any time before midday would be convenient.\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nYours sincerely,\nSara Ahmed" },
  { id: "invitation", tone: "Informal", prompt: "A friend has just moved to the city where you live. Write a letter inviting them to an event next weekend.",
    bullets: ["explain what the event is", "say why you think they will enjoy it", "give details of where and when to meet"],
    answer: "Dear Tom,\n\nIt was great to hear that you've finally moved here – welcome to the city! I hope you're settling into your new flat and that the unpacking isn't taking forever.\n\nI'm writing because there's a food and music festival in Riverside Park next weekend, and I'd love you to come with me. Every year, local restaurants set up stalls along the river, so you can try dishes from all over the world, and there are live bands playing on two stages all afternoon.\n\nI know how much you love trying new food, and you've always been a big fan of live music, so I think it's the perfect way to spend your first weekend here. It would also be a good chance to meet some of my friends, who are really keen to meet you.\n\nShall we meet at the main gate of the park at 1 pm on Saturday? It's a five-minute walk from Central Station. Text me if that time doesn't work for you.\n\nCan't wait to see you!\n\nBest wishes,\nAnna" },
  { id: "job", tone: "Formal", prompt: "You have seen an advertisement for a part-time job at a local museum. Write a letter to the museum manager.",
    bullets: ["say which job you are applying for", "describe your relevant experience and skills", "explain when you are available to work"],
    answer: "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to apply for the position of part-time visitor guide, which was advertised on your museum's website last week.\n\nI am currently in the final year of a history degree at City University, where I have specialised in local and industrial history. Last summer, I worked for three months as a volunteer at the Maritime Heritage Centre, where I led guided tours for groups of up to twenty visitors and helped to run children's activity days. This experience taught me how to explain historical topics clearly to people of all ages. I also speak fluent Spanish and good French, which would allow me to help international visitors.\n\nI am available to work on Saturdays and Sundays throughout the year, as well as on weekday afternoons during university holidays. I would be happy to attend any training sessions that the role requires.\n\nI have attached my CV and two references. Thank you for considering my application, and I look forward to hearing from you.\n\nYours faithfully,\nLucia Romero" },
  { id: "apology", tone: "Informal", prompt: "You recently missed a friend's birthday party. Write a letter to your friend.",
    bullets: ["apologise for missing the party", "explain why you could not attend", "suggest a way to celebrate together"],
    answer: "Dear Priya,\n\nI'm so sorry I missed your birthday party on Saturday. I'd been looking forward to it for weeks, and I feel awful that I wasn't there to celebrate with you.\n\nThe thing is, my grandmother was taken to hospital on Saturday morning after a bad fall. My parents were away, so I had to go with her and stay at the hospital until late in the evening. Luckily, she hadn't broken anything, and she's back home now and feeling much better. I wanted to call you, but my phone battery died, and by the time I got home it was nearly midnight.\n\nTo make up for it, I'd love to take you out for dinner next weekend – my treat! How about that new Italian restaurant near the station that you mentioned? I've also got a present for you that I was going to bring to the party, so I can give it to you then.\n\nLet me know which evening suits you best. Once again, I'm really sorry, and I hope you had a fantastic time.\n\nLots of love,\nMeera" },
];

export type CueCard = { id: string; title: string; points: string[]; answer: string };
export const CUE_CARDS: CueCard[] = [
  { id: "book", title: "Describe a book that you enjoyed reading.", points: ["what the book was", "when you read it", "what it was about", "and explain why you enjoyed it"],
    answer: "I'd like to talk about a novel called The Old Man and the Sea by Ernest Hemingway, which I read about three years ago during my summer holidays. A friend recommended it because it's quite short, so I thought it would be a good way to get back into reading in English.\n\nThe story is about an elderly Cuban fisherman called Santiago, who hasn't caught a fish for eighty-four days. He sails far out to sea alone and finally hooks an enormous marlin, and the book describes his exhausting struggle to bring it back to shore over several days.\n\nWhat I enjoyed most was the way Hemingway writes. His sentences are very simple, but they create a powerful picture of the sea and the old man's determination. I also found the message really inspiring: even though Santiago loses most of the fish to sharks in the end, he never gives up, and he keeps his dignity.\n\nSince then, I've often thought about that book when I've faced difficulties, so it's had a real influence on me, not just as a reader but as a person." },
  { id: "helper", title: "Describe a person who has helped you in your studies or work.", points: ["who the person is", "how you know them", "how they helped you", "and explain how you feel about this person"],
    answer: "I'm going to talk about my maths teacher at secondary school, Mrs Fernandes, who helped me more than anyone else in my studies.\n\nI met her when I was fourteen, which was a difficult time for me because I was really struggling with algebra and my marks were dropping. Instead of just telling me to work harder, she offered to stay behind after class twice a week to go through problems with me step by step.\n\nWhat made her special was her patience. She never made me feel stupid for asking the same question twice, and she had a talent for explaining things using everyday examples, like working out discounts when shopping or sharing a bill at a restaurant. Gradually, the subject started to make sense.\n\nBy the end of the year, my grade had gone up from a C to an A, but more importantly, I'd gained a lot of confidence. In fact, that experience is probably one of the reasons I chose to study engineering later on. I'm still really grateful to her, and we keep in touch even now." },
  { id: "place", title: "Describe a place you would like to visit in the future.", points: ["where it is", "how you know about it", "what you would do there", "and explain why you would like to visit it"],
    answer: "One place I'd really love to visit is Iceland, which I've wanted to see ever since I watched a documentary about it a few years ago.\n\nIceland is a small island country in the North Atlantic, and it's famous for its dramatic landscapes: volcanoes, glaciers, waterfalls and black sand beaches. I'd like to go in winter, because that's the best time to see the northern lights, which is something I've dreamed about since I was a child.\n\nIf I went, I'd probably rent a car and drive around the island on the main ring road, stopping at the huge waterfalls and the hot springs along the way. I'd also love to try a glacier walk with a guide, although I imagine it would be freezing!\n\nThe main reason I want to go is that it seems completely different from anywhere I've been before. I live in a busy city, so the idea of standing in such an empty, wild landscape sounds incredibly peaceful. It's quite an expensive destination, so I'm saving up, and I hope to go within the next couple of years." },
  { id: "skill", title: "Describe a skill that took you a long time to learn.", points: ["what the skill is", "when you started learning it", "how you learned it", "and explain why it took a long time"],
    answer: "I'd like to describe learning to play the guitar, which took me much longer than I expected.\n\nI started when I was about sixteen, after my uncle gave me his old acoustic guitar. At first, I taught myself using free online videos, practising for around half an hour every evening after school.\n\nThe first few months were really frustrating. My fingertips were sore, I couldn't press the strings properly, and changing between chords quickly felt impossible. Several times I nearly gave up. What kept me going was setting small goals, such as learning one new chord a week or playing one complete song by the end of the month. Later, I took lessons with a local teacher, who corrected some bad habits I'd developed.\n\nIt probably took me about two years before I felt genuinely comfortable playing in front of other people. Now I play at family gatherings and occasionally with friends. Looking back, it taught me that progress with any skill is usually slow and uneven, and that consistent practice matters far more than natural talent." },
  { id: "journey", title: "Describe a journey you remember well.", points: ["where you went", "who you went with", "how you travelled", "and explain why you remember it"],
    answer: "The journey I'd like to describe is an overnight train trip I took with my family when I was about twelve, travelling from our city to a coastal town for a summer holiday.\n\nThe journey took around fourteen hours, and we had a sleeper compartment with fold-down bunks, which I found incredibly exciting because I'd never slept on a train before. We brought lots of homemade food, and my brother and I spent hours playing cards and looking out of the window.\n\nWhat made it so memorable was the scenery early the next morning. I woke up at sunrise and saw the train winding along the coast, with the sea on one side and green hills on the other. I remember standing by the door with my father, feeling the breeze and watching fishing boats in the distance.\n\nNowadays, people usually fly to save time, but I think that journey showed me that travelling can be an enjoyable part of a holiday rather than just a way of getting there. Whenever I can, I still choose to travel by train." },
  { id: "technology", title: "Describe a piece of technology you find useful.", points: ["what it is", "when you started using it", "what you use it for", "and explain why you find it useful"],
    answer: "I'd like to talk about the map app on my phone, which I use almost every day.\n\nI first started using it seriously when I moved to a new city for university about four years ago. I didn't know the area at all, so I relied on it to find my way to lectures, shops and friends' houses.\n\nThe app does much more than show a map. It tells me the quickest route by car, bus or on foot, warns me about traffic jams, and even shows how crowded a train is likely to be. When I travel abroad, I download maps in advance so I can use them without an internet connection, which saves a lot of money.\n\nThe main reason I find it so useful is that it saves me time and stress. Before, I would have had to ask strangers for directions or study a paper map, and I'd often get lost. On the other hand, I sometimes worry that I depend on it too much; I'm not sure I could find my way around without it now. Still, overall, it's probably the most practical app I have." },
  { id: "late", title: "Describe a time when you were late for something important.", points: ["what the event was", "why you were late", "what happened as a result", "and explain how you felt about it"],
    answer: "I'd like to describe the day I was late for a job interview, about two years ago.\n\nIt was for a position at a marketing company that I really wanted to work for, and the interview was at ten o'clock in the city centre. I'd planned to take the train, leaving myself about thirty minutes to spare.\n\nUnfortunately, that morning the train was cancelled because of a signal failure, and the next one was so crowded that I couldn't get on. I tried to book a taxi, but the traffic was terrible, and in the end I arrived about twenty minutes late, feeling hot and stressed.\n\nLuckily, I'd called the company as soon as I knew there was a problem, and the interviewer was very understanding. She said she appreciated that I had let them know, and the interview went well. In fact, I got the job!\n\nThe experience taught me two things: always leave more time than you think you need for important events, and if something goes wrong, communicate early. Being honest made a far better impression than making excuses." },
  { id: "festival", title: "Describe a celebration or festival that is important in your country.", points: ["what it is", "when it happens", "what people do", "and explain why it is important"],
    answer: "I'd like to talk about the New Year celebration in my country, which is the most important festival of the year for most families.\n\nPreparations usually begin a week or two before. People clean their houses thoroughly, buy new clothes and decorate their homes with lights and flowers. On New Year's Eve, the whole extended family gathers at my grandparents' house for a big dinner, and everyone helps to cook traditional dishes.\n\nAt midnight, we go out into the street to watch fireworks with our neighbours, and children receive small gifts or money from older relatives. The next day is usually quiet, and many people visit friends or go for a walk in the park.\n\nFor me, the festival is important because it's one of the few times when all my relatives are together in one place. My cousins live in different cities now, so it's a rare chance to catch up. It also feels like a fresh start, so many people make resolutions for the coming year. I think traditions like this help families stay close even when life gets busy." },
];

